# Python

General rules: [CODE_STYLE.md](./CODE_STYLE.md).

## Tools

| Tool | Checks | Where it runs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) | virtual environments, dependencies, lock file | locally, CI |
| `ruff check` | lint (replaces flake8, isort, pyupgrade and more) | CI `python-* / check` |
| `ruff format` | formatting | pre-commit hook, the AI assistant's format hook, CI |
| `pytest` | tests | CI `python-* / check` |
| `pip-audit` | vulnerable dependencies | CI `python-* / audit` (does not block merging) |
| Type checker (mypy or pyright, optional) | types | add it to the project's `test_command` |

Configuration lives in `pyproject.toml` (`[tool.ruff]`, `[tool.pytest.ini_options]`).

## Dependencies

- Manage dependencies with `uv add` / `uv add --dev` and **commit `uv.lock`**.
- Keep production and development dependencies separate.
- A new dependency needs a stated reason (see [CI_QUALITY.md](./CI_QUALITY.md#dependencies)).

## Code

- New code has type hints; public functions must.
- Naming: modules and functions `snake_case`, classes `PascalCase`, constants `UPPER_CASE`.
- No mutable default arguments.
- Use `pathlib` for paths and timezone-aware `datetime` (UTC) for times.

## Exceptions

- Each module defines its domain exceptions on a shared base class.
- No bare `except:`, and never catch `Exception` only to ignore it.
- Keep the cause when re-raising: `raise BillingError("refund failed") from exc`.
- At the boundary (HTTP handler, task entry point), map exceptions to responses in one place; log only unknown exceptions at ERROR and return a fixed message.

## Logging

- Structured logging (the standard `logging` module with a JSON formatter, or `structlog`).
- Messages are fixed phrases with the varying values as fields: `logger.info("invoice paid", extra={"invoice_id": id})`.
- `request_id` and `trace_id` are added automatically (contextvars or the OpenTelemetry integration).
- Never log secrets, tokens or full request bodies.

## Concurrency

- No blocking I/O in async code; when unavoidable, use `asyncio.to_thread`.
- Background tasks have a clear lifecycle and can be cancelled.

## Testing

- `pytest`, tests in `tests/`, files named `test_*.py`.
- Prepare data with fixtures; fake outbound HTTP with `respx`, `responses` or similar — no network.
- A bug fix starts with a test that reproduces the bug.
